॰ ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
॰ THE STATEMENT SPLITTER — D-002f3, the port of parse.rs:481-675.
॰
॰ D-002f1 declared every record and every appender and READ NO TOKEN, and
॰ said so at (b) below. D-002f10 then filled चिह्नककोश — पदविभाग writes
॰ real tokens now, append_sites 2, kinds_produced 6 — so the arena this
॰ row was BLOCKED IN PRACTICE on is no longer empty. This block is the
॰ walk over it.
॰
॰ ── WHAT parse.rs's TOKEN LOOP DOES, AND WHAT IS PORTED ──
॰ Statements are daṇḍa-terminated rather than line-terminated, so an
॰ instruction written across two lines stays ONE sentence. ॥ is a PAIRED
॰ delimiter and not a terminator (ADR-0012): which of the two a given ॥
॰ is depends on whether a directive is already open, and an unpaired one
॰ must NOT be allowed to swallow the directive after it — so words before
॰ an unopened ॥ are reported (P24) rather than treated as an opener,
॰ because opening here would report a SECOND fault at end of file and
॰ leave the reader two diagnostics for one omission. A label ends at its
॰ ॱॱ and not at a daṇḍa: it is not a sentence, it names the place where
॰ the next one begins.
॰
॰ ── ON THE STATEMENT, WHICH IS A RANGE AND NOT A VECTOR ──
॰ Rust accumulates `statement: Vec<&Token>` and clears it at every
॰ delimiter. T1 HAS NO SLICE CONSTRUCTOR AND NO ITERATOR — the language
॰ fact D-002f10 was rooted in — so the accumulator here is the PAIR
॰ (वाक्यारम्भः ऽ वाक्यसीमा), half-open indices into चिह्नककोश, which is
॰ सङ्केतन's (पाठ्यम् ऽ आरम्भः ऽ सीमा) convention at अष्टकान्वेषणम् applied
॰ to an arena rather than to text. That costs nothing here and is not an
॰ approximation: every delimiter clears, and every non-delimiter appends,
॰ so what Rust holds in the Vec is ALWAYS a contiguous run of the arena.
॰ The statement is empty exactly when वाक्यारम्भः समम् वाक्यसीमा.
॰
॰ Rust's `opened: Option<&Token>` is उद्घाटकः, an arena INDEX, with ०
॰ meaning none — this file's ABSENT, stated at the head.
॰
॰ ── ON THE THREE RECORDS THAT GAINED A NAME RANGE ──
॰ वाक्य ॱ नाम, चिह्न ॱ नाम and पाठांश ॱ पाठ are each अङ्कः अन्तः अ८ and
॰ were written by D-002f1 against Rust's String. A token's text cannot be
॰ CUT out of the source here, so each of the three gains आरम्भ and सीमा
॰ and नाम/पाठ becomes the text the name lies IN. That is exactly the move
॰ lex.t1 made this morning when चिह्नक gained पाठसीमा, and it is an
॰ EXTENSION and not an alteration: no field lost a name or a type, so
॰ every field the port's own test pins is still there and still means
॰ what it meant. Without it L02 could not be written at all — comparing
॰ a global to a label would compare two handles to the same whole source
॰ and report every global defined.
॰
॰ ── ON THE DIAGNOSTIC CODES, WHICH ARE OCTET RUNS ──
॰ P01, P24, L02 are LATIN, and doc 15's repertoire refuses a Latin letter
॰ — the same wall वाक्यभेदनाम is stubbed against and the one nidana.t1
॰ names at निदानपङ्क्तिः (*"the कूट arrives as an अष्टक run from the
॰ caller"*). T1 has no character literal and no array literal, so each
॰ code is BUILT as its octets by कूटरचना, exactly as lex.t1 writes every
॰ sign as its UTF-8 octets rather than as a character. 'P' is ८०, 'L' is
॰ ७६ and the digits are ४८ upward. This is the only way a code can exist
॰ in a T1 source today; it is not a workaround for this row.
॰ ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

॰ the eight codes this row raises, each three octets. Built by कूटरचना.
