॰ ══ THE OCTET APPENDER'S OWN STATE, USED AND NEVER DECLARED ══
॰
॰ `अष्टकयोजनम्`:2770 advances this cursor and writes at it — the ADVANCE-THEN-
॰ WRITE appender idiom every arena in this crate uses — and neither the cursor
॰ nor its arena existed. The five `…पाठः` runs below are filled by
॰ `अङ्कपाठरचना` a few lines further on, one octet at a time.
॰
॰ TYPES READ FROM THE USE, NOT GUESSED. The cursor is `अ६४` because
॰ `अष्टकयोजनम्` returns `अ६४` and the body assigns `… योगः १` to it; the runs
॰ are `अङ्कः अन्तः अ८` because each is written `अङ्कः n अन्तः भवति २२४`, a
॰ single octet.
॰
॰ `भवति ०` AT A SLICE TYPE IS THE EMPTY RUN, matching the fifteen error codes
॰ above and every other run global in this file. WHETHER ONE SLOT IS ENOUGH FOR
॰ A WRITE AT INDEX १ IS A RUNTIME QUESTION AND IS NOT SETTLED HERE: the resolve
॰ census asks whether a name is in scope, not whether an arena is long enough.
॰ Saying so because a declaration that merely silences a pass, while leaving a
॰ bound wrong, is the kind of green this tree has been bitten by.
॰ `अष्टकसञ्चयसूचकाङ्कः` and `अष्टकसञ्चयः` STOOD HERE UNTIL 2026-09-04 (W-239):
॰ the octet store and its cursor are `अष्टकॱअष्टककोश` / `अष्टकॱअष्टकसूचकाङ्क` now,
॰ one arena for the whole program, one-based as this stash was.
